            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the German for to recycle, then the German for to consume."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: die Bedingung",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "die Bedingung — \"the condition\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ich komme mit, unter einer Bedingung: Du bezahlst das Taxi. I will come along on one condition: you pay for the taxi."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Plural: die Bedingungen. Unter der Bedingung, dass … — on condition that …"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Bedingung is the noun of bedingen \"to stipulate\", inherited from Middle High German bedingen \"to hire, to engage\" (EtymDB). The verb is usually explained as built on Ding in its old sense of a court or assembly, Old High German ding, Proto-Germanic þingą (EtymDB): terms agreed before the assembly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
